{
 "p6": {
  "labels": {
   "sig": "Colour signature",
   "type": "SoT type",
   "pres": "Generators of 632, and what each does to the colours",
   "flag": "● in the stabiliser",
   "seatSym": "S(𝒫|M)"
  },
  "caption": "Arcs: the three generators chosen for 632, each a turn about one centre; open dot, it moves colours; filled dot, it keeps them all. Red: this turn is in the stabiliser of the ringed copy. Colour chips give its colour cycle. Translations are not drawn.",
  "typeExt": " this page’s extension: SoT’s tables stop at prime k",
  "relationsNote": " Read right to left: αβγ means γ first, then β, then α. The Symmetries of Things (p. 171) and Vladimir Bulatov’s catalog read left to right. Any two of the three generate Γ: β = α⁻¹γ.",
  "explainer": [
   [
    "Pattern",
    "Disjoint congruent copies of one motif, where a symmetry of the whole set can carry any copy to any other (<a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"http://intel/books/tilings-and-patterns.pdf#page=215\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“an isometry of the plane that maps P onto itself and Mᵢ onto Mⱼ”\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 204\" data-private=\"1\">T&amp;P p. 204</a>). Generators are not in the definition: they are only one way to write the symmetry group."
   ],
   [
    "Stabiliser",
    "The symmetries that map one given copy onto itself: T&amp;P’s induced group S(𝒫|M) (<a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"http://intel/books/tilings-and-patterns.pdf#page=215\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“sometimes S(P∣M) is called the stabilizer of M in S(P)”\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 204\" data-private=\"1\">p. 204</a>). It belongs to the copy, not to a generator list. Every copy has its own, all conjugate."
   ],
   [
    "Colouring",
    "Here always perfect: every symmetry permutes the colours (<a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"https://yaroslavvb.github.io/animated-groups/Grunbaum%20B.%2C%20Shephard%20G%20%281990%29%20Tilings%20and%20Patterns%20%28Color%20Patterns%29.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“every symmetry of P is associated with a color symmetry”\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 404\">T&amp;P p. 404</a>). H is the group keeping one colour (<a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"http://intel/animated-groups/Conway%20J.%2C%20Goodman-Strauss%20C.%20%282008%29%20The%20Symmetries%20of%20Things.pdf#page=174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“This group is called the stabilizer of that color.”\" data-src=\"Conway, Burgiel &amp; Goodman-Strauss, The Symmetries of Things, p. 155\" data-private=\"1\">SoT p. 155</a>), and the colours are its cosets gH."
   ],
   [
    "Placement",
    "If H is not normal it has conjugates, and each is a different colouring of the same copies. A motif in general position allows all [Γ : N(H)] of them; a motif on a centre allows only those containing its stabiliser. For p6[3]₂, T&amp;P print them in <a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"https://yaroslavvb.github.io/animated-groups/Grunbaum%20B.%2C%20Shephard%20G%20%281990%29%20Tilings%20and%20Patterns%20%28Color%20Patterns%29.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"The card p6[3]₂, PP42[3]₂ of this figure is placement C below.\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 416\">Fig. 8.2.3</a> and <a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"https://yaroslavvb.github.io/animated-groups/Grunbaum%20B.%2C%20Shephard%20G%20%281990%29%20Tilings%20and%20Patterns%20%28Color%20Patterns%29.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“the patterns differ in the choice of motif that bears color 1”\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 420\">Fig. 8.2.4(b)</a>; the word is Vladimir Bulatov’s."
   ],
   [
    "More",
    "<a class=\"source-value-link\" href=\"https://spacesheep.dev/@yaroslavvb/patterns-orbits-cosets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A friendly tutorial: pattern, stabiliser, orbit, coset ↗</a>. Book references here: hover or tap for the words, click for the PDF at that page. Links into T&amp;P’s chapter 8 open for anyone; links to T&amp;P’s chapter 5 and to SoT open only on Yaroslav’s machines, so other readers get the words."
   ]
  ],
  "explainerMore": [
   [
    "Seat",
    "Where the motif sits among the rotation centres: general, 2-, 3- or 6-fold. It decides the stabiliser. This page’s word; crystallographers say Wyckoff position."
   ],
   [
    "Pattern type",
    "A classification, not a picture (<a class=\"book-ref-link source-value-link\" href=\"https://yaroslavvb.github.io/animated-groups/Grunbaum%20B.%2C%20Shephard%20G%20%281990%29%20Tilings%20and%20Patterns%20%28Color%20Patterns%29.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-words=\"“the same stabilizers of their colored motifs”\" data-src=\"Grünbaum &amp; Shephard, Tilings and Patterns, p. 421\">T&amp;P p. 421</a>): same colour group, same stabilisers of coloured motifs, same motif-transitive subgroups. One type can have several different-looking pictures."
   ]
  ],
